<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="page4-best-response" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Finding Nash Equilibria Using Best Response</title>

  <p>
    We start with a reading to explore another way to find Nash equilibria.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-3-8">
    <title>Section 3.8, Finding Nash Equilibria Using Best Response</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 3.8.</alert>
    </p>

    <p>
      An equivalent way to think of a strategy profile that is a Nash
      equilibrium is that each player's strategy is a <em>best response</em>
      to the rest of the strategy profile.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Before moving on, let's make sure we understand both the conceptual meaning of this and the notation!
  </p>


  <exercise xml:id="check-best-response-words">
    <statement>
      <p>
        In words, what is a best response for player <m>i</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Hold the other players' strategies fixed. Player <m>i</m>'s best
            response is <em>the</em> strategy giving player <m>i</m> the
            highest payoff against it.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Almost, but the word <q>the</q> is misleading. Nothing stops two
            strategies from tying for the highest payoff, in which case both
            are best responses. You will see exactly this happen in the next
            section, and it is the reason <m>B_i(s_{-i})</m> is a set.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Hold the other players' strategies fixed. Player <m>i</m>'s best
            responses to that are the strategies giving player <m>i</m> the
            highest payoff available against it, and there may be more than one
            if some of them tie.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Both halves matter: a best response only makes sense
            relative to a fixed <m>s_{-i}</m>, and there can be several of
            them at once.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The strategy that gives player <m>i</m> the highest payoff no
            matter what the other players do.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            That is a dominant strategy, which is a much stronger condition and
            usually does not exist. A best response is always a response
            <em>to something</em>; change what the other players are doing and
            player <m>i</m>'s best response can change with it.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>
  <p>
    It is also worth reviewing the notation <m>s_{-i}</m> from earlier in this chapter! It means
    the strategies of everybody <em>except</em> player <m>i</m>.
    So when we examine a Nash equilibrium <m>(s_1^*, s_2^*, \ldots, s_n^*)</m>,
    we have <m>s^*_{-i} = (s_1^*, \ldots, s_{i-1}^*, s_{i+1}^*, \ldots, s_n^*)</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Furthermore, we can use a more concise notation for best response! For example,
    <m>B_1(s_{-1})</m> denotes the set of player 1's best responses when the other
    players' strategies are fixed at some specific profile <m>s_{-1}</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    With this notation, we can write the book's formulation of Nash equilibrium in terms of best response even more concisely! That is,
    <m>s^* = (s_1^*, \ldots, s_n^*)</m> is a Nash equilibrium if and only if
    <me>s_i^* \in B_i(s^*_{-i}) \text{ for every player } i = 1, \ldots, n.</me>
  </p>


  <exercise xml:id="check-best-response-nash">
    <statement>
      <p>
        In words, how does best response connect to Nash equilibrium?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>s^*</m> is a Nash equilibrium exactly when every player's
            strategy <m>s_i^*</m> is a best response to <m>s_{-i}</m> for
            every possible choice <m>s_{-i}</m> by the other players.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            This is the dominant strategy condition again, and it is much
            stronger than what a Nash equilibrium requires. The Nash condition
            only asks about one particular <m>s_{-i}</m>, namely
            <m>s^*_{-i}</m>. This is the same distinction the checkpoint on
            page 1 was drawing, now in best response language.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>s^*</m> is a Nash equilibrium exactly when at least one player's
            strategy <m>s_i^*</m> is a best response to <m>s^*_{-i}</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not enough. If only some players are best responding, the ones who
            are not can improve their payoff by switching, and that is exactly
            what a Nash equilibrium forbids. Every player's condition has to
            hold simultaneously.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>s^*</m> is a Nash equilibrium exactly when every player's
            strategy <m>s_i^*</m> is a best response to the other players'
            strategies <m>s^*_{-i}</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct, and note that it has to hold for <em>every</em> player at
            the same time. One player being happy is not enough.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>


  <p>
    Now for a quick example from the book demonstrating this.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-3-9">
    <title>Section 3.9, Big Monkey and Little Monkey 5</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 3.9.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      This is the same matrix you have now seen in Chapter 2 and on the last
      page, so you can spend your attention on the boxes rather than the game.
      Pay attention to how the boxes are drawn, because your homework will ask
      you to analyze games in this way.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    For reference, here is the matrix once more (unboxed).
  </p>

  <table xml:id="table-bmlm-best-response">
    <title>Big Monkey and Little Monkey in normal form, Big Monkey first</title>
    <tabular halign="center">
      <row header="yes">
        <cell></cell>
        <cell>ww</cell>
        <cell>wc</cell>
        <cell>cw</cell>
        <cell>cc</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>w</cell>
        <cell>(0, 0)</cell>
        <cell>(0, 0)</cell>
        <cell>(9, 1)</cell>
        <cell>(9, 1)</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>c</cell>
        <cell>(4, 4)</cell>
        <cell>(5, 3)</cell>
        <cell>(4, 4)</cell>
        <cell>(5, 3)</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>


  <p>
    The main thing to be careful about is the bookkeeping: whether you read
    across a row or down a column, and which entry of each payoff pair you
    compare. It helps to first ask yourself whose best response you are
    finding. You move through <em>that</em> player's options while the other
    player's strategy stays fixed, and you compare only <em>that</em> player's
    payoffs.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-box-big-monkey">
    <statement>
      <p>
        To find Big Monkey's best response to Little Monkey's strategy
        <m>cw</m>, what do you do, and what do you box?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Look down the <m>cw</m> column, compare Big Monkey's payoffs
            <m>9</m> and <m>4</m>, and box the <m>9</m> in the <m>w</m> row.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Fixing Little Monkey's strategy means fixing a column,
            and Big Monkey's payoffs are the first entries. He waits, so the
            <m>9</m> gets boxed.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Look across the <m>w</m> row, compare Big Monkey's payoffs
            <m>0, 0, 9, 9</m>, and box the two <m>9</m>s.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            This compares Big Monkey's payoff against four different things
            Little Monkey might do, which is not a best response question at
            all. A best response requires the other player's strategy to be
            held fixed, and here that means staying inside a single column.
            Reading across a row is how you find <em>Little Monkey's</em> best
            responses.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Look down the <m>cw</m> column, compare the payoffs <m>1</m> and
            <m>4</m>, and box the <m>4</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Right column, wrong entries. Those are Little Monkey's payoffs, the
            second numbers in each pair. Big Monkey's are the first numbers, so
            the comparison is <m>9</m> against <m>4</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Now let's check that you understand how the <q>best response boxes</q>
    relate to Nash equilibria.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-all-boxed">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Once all the best response boxes are drawn in a matrix, how can you
        tell which cells are Nash equilibria?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            A cell is a Nash equilibrium when at least one payoff in it is
            boxed.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not enough. An unboxed payoff means that player is not best
            responding, so they would rather switch. For example, in
            <m>(c, wc)</m> Big Monkey's <m>5</m> is boxed, but Little Monkey's
            <m>3</m> is not, and she would rather switch to <m>ww</m> or
            <m>cw</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            A cell is a Nash equilibrium exactly when <em>every</em> payoff in
            it is boxed.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. A boxed payoff means that player is best responding to
            what the others are doing in that cell, so a fully boxed cell is
            one where every player is best responding at once.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            A cell is a Nash equilibrium when its boxed payoffs are the largest
            ones in the matrix.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The size of the payoffs is not what matters. For example,
            <m>(c, ww)</m> and <m>(c, cw)</m> both have payoffs <m>(4, 4)</m>,
            but only <m>(c, ww)</m> is a Nash equilibrium: in the <m>cw</m>
            column, Big Monkey's <m>4</m> loses to the <m>9</m> above it, so
            it is not boxed. The payoffs alone cannot tell you; the boxes can.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    This works because a boxed payoff means that player is best responding to
    the other player's strategy. So a cell with every payoff boxed is one where
    <em>every</em> player is best responding at the same time, which is exactly
    our condition <m>s_i^* \in B_i(s^*_{-i})</m> for every player <m>i</m>,
    drawn on a matrix.
  </p>

  <p>
    Time to read another example, this time with three players!
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-3-10">
    <title>Section 3.10, Water Pollution 2</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 3.10.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      This is the same technique with three players instead of two, so it
      takes some more careful bookkeeping. See if you can do the boxing
      yourself on Table 3.1, then check your work against the book's
      Table 3.2.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    The only part of the method that should feel different here is Firm 3.
    Once you fix strategies for Firm 1 and Firm 2, you compare Firm 3's
    payoffs in that same cell position across the two tables (moving between
    the two tables while staying in a fixed position is how you change
    Firm 3's strategy!!).
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-3-11">
    <title>Section 3.11, Cournot Duopoly</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Skim Section 3.11.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      You can read this one as carefully (or not!) as you like. It explores
      best responses and Nash equilibria in the context of a continuous game,
      but we aren't going into this topic right now.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-3-4" iframe="question-form.html?section=3.4" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>